# compiled-loop-with-empty-body — a WHILE with no body breaks once compiled

STATUS: DONE in 2.0.21. Found 2026-08-30 while open-coding REPEAT and
FOREACH: a test written for the NEW code's empty-body case failed, and the
same shape turned out to be already in the OLD code for WHILE.

## Symptom

A `while` with no body — idiomatic AutoLISP for draining a list —

    (defun drain (l / i) (setq i l) (while (setq i (cdr i))) i)

works interpreted and CRASHES once the function is compiled:

    A compound form was expected, but NIL found.
    current LOOP context: DO NIL.

It is a Common Lisp error, not an AutoLISP one, raised when the function
is CALLED rather than when it is compiled: SBCL compiles the broken form
into a function that signals COMPILED-PROGRAM-ERROR on entry. So the
symptom is a host-level error from a program that had been running fine,
appearing the moment the function got hot enough to compile.

## Cause

TRANSPILE-BODY yields NIL for an empty body, and the WHILE branch spliced
it straight into a LOOP:

    (loop while <test> do ,(transpile-body (rest arguments) context-var))

With no body that is `(loop while X do nil)`, and LOOP's DO requires a
COMPOUND form. NIL is not one.

Nothing caught it because no entry in the equivalence corpus had an empty
loop body — the corpus is what the compiler is checked against, and it
only checks the shapes it contains.

## Fix

pjb's, and better than the one first written. The first attempt wrapped
the body at each site that uses it as a statement -- `do (progn ...)` in
WHILE and in the REPEAT this slice added, which had inherited the same
shape by being written to look like it. That works and leaves a rule
every future loop has to remember.

His: make TRANSPILE-BODY return `(PROGN)` for an empty body instead of
NIL. (PROGN) evaluates to NIL, so no value changes; what changes is that
a transpiled body is ALWAYS A COMPOUND FORM, and can therefore go
anywhere a form can. The use sites go back to splicing it plainly, and
there is nothing left to remember.

The difference is where the invariant lives: at one definition, or in the
head of whoever writes the next loop.

## Tests

Empty-body entries for WHILE, REPEAT and FOREACH added to the corpora, so
the shape is checked rather than assumed to be uninteresting. Verified
they fail without the fix.

## The general point

The bug lived in the difference between "a body" and "a body with at
least one form" — a case that reads as degenerate and is ordinary in
AutoLISP, where `(while (setq i (cdr i)))` is how you walk a list for its
side effect. Worth remembering when adding any further loop: the empty
body is not an edge case here, it is an idiom.
